  "response": "Attribution in a collapse inquiry proceeds by reconstructing the load path three times over \u2014 as designed, as detailed on the shop drawings, and as finally erected \u2014 and then asking at which transition capacity dropped below demand. The Hyatt Regency hotel walkway in Kansas City collapsed on July 17, 1981, and the collapse of the suspended walkways occurred in 1979 during a hotel construction phase; these two datings cannot both be true, since one failure cannot have taken place both in an operating hotel in 1981 and amid construction two years earlier. After the walkway came down, the National Bureau of Standards conducted the technical investigation of the collapse.\n\nThe wreckage is interrogated first. Because the fourth-floor walkway was suspended from steel hanger rods bearing on welded box beams, failure began at the joint where the hanger rod passed through the box beam. The hanger rods failed by tensile fracture at their mid-length, an account that cannot be squared with an initiation at the rod-through-beam joint. Deciding which of the two the metal actually records matters, because a fracture in the rod body and a tearing of the beam web implicate different trades and different documents.\n\nThe documentary record is then laid against the physical one, and it is here that design intent and construction reality are compared detail by detail. The original design called for a single continuous hanger rod running from the roof framing through the fourth-floor walkway to the second-floor walkway, and the as-built arrangement used two offset rods in place of one continuous rod; the rod prescribed on the design drawings and the rod actually hung in the atrium cannot both have been the configuration in service. Because the offset pair was built where the continuous rod had been specified, the fourth-floor box beam connection came to carry twice the load. A connection sized for the demand of a single walkway was therefore asked to deliver the demand of two, which is precisely the kind of discrepancy that distinguishes an error committed on the drawing board from one committed in the field.\n\nWhereas the continuous-rod detail was the design office's own prescription, the impetus for the substitution came from a different quarter. The steel fabricator initiated the request to substitute the two-rod detail. The engineer of record's firm affixed its review stamp to the shop drawings showing the two-rod detail. In the ordinary case, such a sequence is what allows responsibility to be apportioned, since a reviewer's stamp converts a fabricator's convenience into an engineered decision.\n\nInvestigators performed laboratory load tests on replica box beam and hanger rod assemblies. Investigators computed the ultimate capacity of the connection and compared it with the loads acting at the time of collapse. Taken together, the doubling of the load at the fourth-floor box beam connection and the measured behaviour of the replica assemblies show that the final investigation report determined that the connection as built could not carry the loads mandated by the applicable building code. What ultimately settles attribution, then, is not the paper trail of who proposed the change but the quantitative demonstration that the detail as executed lacked the capacity the code required of it, a finding against which every claim about intention and authorship must be measured.",
